23:46:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes, exactly! Those reviews are absolutely talking about a fundamentally different system—one where the bullet **really is** a simulated object moving through the game world, and its trajectory is tracked frame by frame, with physics applied. In games like PUBG and Arma, you’re dealing with a **projectile simulation** model rather than hitscan, and that’s the core feature they’re bragging about. --- ### 1. The bullet becomes a real, persistent object When you pull the trigger, the game server (and your client, for smoothness) spawns a tiny invisible projectile. - It has an initial velocity. - Each game tick (e.g., 60 times per second), the game moves it forward based on its velocity. - It then applies **gravity** (pulling it downward) and possibly **drag** (air resistance slowing it down) to its velocity, just like a real round. - Every tick, it checks if the bullet’s current position has collided with terrain, a wall, or a player. If it hits, it deals damage at that exact point. So yes—the trajectory really is tracked. The bullet doesn’t just teleport a ray to a target; it actually travels, arcs over distances, and takes time to get there. That’s why you have to lead moving targets and compensate for drop at long range. --- ### 2. How this works in a multiplayer server context This adds complexity compared to hitscan, but the principle is still “server is the referee.” - Your client sends a fire packet to the server: “I fired a bullet of type X from position Y with initial direction Z at time T.” - The authoritative server spawns that exact projectile into its own simulation. It then advances it each server tick, applying the same physics. - To keep things looking smooth, your client also spawns a **local prediction** of the same projectile immediately. It predicts where it will go based on your aim, gravity, etc. That’s why you see the bullet trace immediately, just like a hitscan tracer, but this time the tracer’s movement is actually driven by a simulated position. - The server’s version is the truth. If, due to lag, your client’s prediction ends up in a slightly different place (e.g., you fired while strafing and the server’s projectile spawns from a slightly corrected position), the server’s hit or miss result will override your local one. For the shooter, this is usually invisible because the discrepancies are tiny. For the target, they see the true server‑authoritative projectile arrive. --- ### 3. Tracers finally aren’t lies (or are much less of one) In PUBG, the glowing streak you see *is* typically attached to the server‑authoritative (or locally predicted, then reconciled) projectile’s position. It’s not a pre‑calculated flash—it’s a visual trailing the actual bullet object. If the bullet arcs, the tracer arcs. If the bullet takes 0.5 seconds to reach its target, the tracer lasts that long. That’s the “realistic travel time” they’re bragging about. Arma takes it even further: rounds might ricochet, penetrate different materials, or fragment, and all of that is simulated on the trajectory. The bullet object literally checks what type of surface it hits and reacts accordingly. --- ### In short Those articles are telling you that those games **don’t** use the “instant math trick.” They use a full physical simulation for each bullet, with real trajectory tracking across multiple server ticks. It’s computationally more expensive and harder to network, but it’s what makes long‑range engagements feel so weighty and skill‑based, because you have to physically compensate for time and drop rather than just aim directly at a pixel.
